  // -- combat one-shots (41, v2 in 43, materials + layered crit in 44) --------
  // Kevin's review of v2: the swing stopped sounding like a swing, the hit
  // sounded like a punch rather than a sword on armour, and the crit was dull,
  // "like you dropped a trash can". Three changes came out of that.
  //
  // SWING is a MISS. A whiff is air and nothing else, so the prompt forbids
  // contact three ways and the take was chosen on shape: the winner peaks
  // 31 percent of the way in (a swell), not at sample zero, which is what an
  // impact looks like. Every v2 candidate that "sounded powerful" was
  // front-loaded, i.e. it had a hit in it.
  //
  // HITS ARE PER MATERIAL. One impact cannot be right for a rat and for plate
  // armour. Three samples, picked at the call site by hitMat_(): flesh for
  // bare creatures, leather for goblins and lightly geared players, plate for
  // anyone in metal. Nothing in the bestiary wears armour today, so flesh and
  // leather are what actually play; plate is here so an armoured enemy added
  // later only has to declare mat:'plate'.
  //
  // CRIT IS A LAYER, NOT A BIGGER THUD. A crit that is only louder never feels
  // special, and the v2 attempt proved it: all low-mid, no top, no sustain.
  // So the crit plays the material impact and lays combat-crit-ring over it
  // 15 ms later, a bright blade singing. The ring carries almost no energy
  // below 300 Hz (measured 0.002 of total), so it adds shine without mudding
  // the impact, and the crit reads the same however armoured the target is.
  //
  // Generated with harness/sfx.py prompts (ElevenLabs, SIX takes each, winners
  // chosen on measured peak position, decay, spectral centroid and share of
  // energy above 2 kHz, then confirmed on spectrograms). 96k mono, peak
  // -1 dBFS. heavy and parry are still the v1 takes Kevin approved; block is
  // the v2 take. Repetition is handled by a random detune per play.
